1
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:15,367
Chief! So cold! I open my closet,

2
00:00:15,367 --> 00:00:17,967
and my down jacket is cold!

3
00:00:17,967 --> 00:00:21,527
Isn't it supposed to
be warm? Why's it cold?

4
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:26,543
Easy now. Feel your own
hand. It's warm, right?

5
00:00:26,900 --> 00:00:30,500
Warm. But it's not plugged in,

6
00:00:30,500 --> 00:00:33,767
so why's it warm once I wear it?

7
00:00:33,767 --> 00:00:36,307
Where does the heat come from?

8
00:00:36,767 --> 00:00:40,107
Hmm, the answer's closer than you think.

9
00:00:40,667 --> 00:00:43,747
Guess: wrap ice in a down jacket.

10
00:00:44,533 --> 00:00:47,913
Will it melt faster, or slower?

11
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:52,637
Why does the park's iron railing feel
so much colder than the wooden bench?

12
00:00:54,533 --> 00:00:58,983
And why does a wet down jacket
stop being warm so fast?

13
00:00:59,400 --> 00:01:03,100
A cold jacket... why's it warm on me?

14
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:07,200
Try this: put a thermometer
in a down jacket overnight.

15
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,360
By morning: about room temp.

16
00:01:12,133 --> 00:01:14,063
It's not warmer?

17
00:01:14,867 --> 00:01:18,187
So it's as cold as the chair in the room?

18
00:01:18,967 --> 00:01:24,867
Yes. Chairs, tables, this jacket:
given time, they match the room.

19
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:27,587
Colder than your hand, so they feel cold.

20
00:01:28,833 --> 00:01:31,593
Something warm must be inside!

21
00:01:32,133 --> 00:01:35,533
Fair guess. It really
feels like it makes heat.

22
00:01:36,433 --> 00:01:40,833
But open it up: just
down and fabric inside.

23
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:43,653
If it made heat, the
thermometer would've shown it.

24
00:01:45,367 --> 00:01:47,997
So where IS the heater?

25
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:49,230
You.

26
00:01:49,300 --> 00:01:51,430
You're the heater yourself.

27
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,310
Me? I'm not burning a fire.

28
00:01:55,733 --> 00:01:57,933
Your body uses what you eat.

29
00:01:58,800 --> 00:02:01,970
So eating is like adding firewood?

30
00:02:02,433 --> 00:02:04,100
Sort of, yes.

31
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:07,300
Most of the energy it
uses ends up as heat.

32
00:02:08,267 --> 00:02:12,637
An adult sitting quietly gives
off about 100 watts of heat,

33
00:02:13,533 --> 00:02:15,963
like an old-style light bulb.

34
00:02:16,733 --> 00:02:19,443
So I'm a light bulb too?

35
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:22,670
Kids are smaller and give off less,

36
00:02:23,467 --> 00:02:27,847
but you never stop. Even asleep,
you just give off a bit less.

37
00:02:29,567 --> 00:02:32,317
What about when I'm shivering with cold?

38
00:02:33,467 --> 00:02:35,257
Your body's turning it up.

39
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:39,660
Shivering can make several
times the heat of sitting still.

40
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,350
Next time you shiver, tell yourself:

41
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:47,323
My heater's working overtime.

42
00:02:48,133 --> 00:02:50,733
But I make heat all day,

43
00:02:50,733 --> 00:02:53,600
so why do I still get cold?

44
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:55,140
Rule of heat:

45
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:57,710
it always flows from hot to cold.

46
00:02:59,533 --> 00:03:03,843
Hold hot cocoa: heat flows
from the cup into your hands.

47
00:03:04,967 --> 00:03:08,387
Hold ice: your hand gets
cold and the ice melts.

48
00:03:09,533 --> 00:03:12,513
So the ice gives me its cold?

49
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:17,210
The other way: your hand's
heat goes into the ice.

50
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:20,157
Cold doesn't move. Heat is what moves.

51
00:03:21,267 --> 00:03:24,267
Can't heat flow backward?

52
00:03:24,267 --> 00:03:28,057
Not on its own. Ice never
makes your hand warmer.

53
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:33,540
On cold tiles barefoot, heat
leaves your feet for the floor.

54
00:03:34,700 --> 00:03:36,690
But rugs feel okay,

55
00:03:37,033 --> 00:03:39,767
so why are tiles colder?

56
00:03:39,767 --> 00:03:42,367
Hold that thought. The park will tell.

57
00:03:43,367 --> 00:03:47,500
So cold is just heat always running away?

58
00:03:47,500 --> 00:03:50,690
Right. Clothes can't
stop it, only slow it.

59
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:55,080
The iron railing's freezing!

60
00:03:55,200 --> 00:03:57,590
The wooden bench is fine.

61
00:03:58,567 --> 00:04:01,087
Iron's just colder, right?

62
00:04:01,367 --> 00:04:05,847
Sounds right. But they spent
all night in the same park,

63
00:04:06,433 --> 00:04:08,783
so they're about equally cold.

64
00:04:09,800 --> 00:04:13,360
No way! They feel so different.

65
00:04:14,533 --> 00:04:16,303
Hands fool you.

66
00:04:16,500 --> 00:04:19,280
Your hand feels how fast heat leaves it.

67
00:04:21,100 --> 00:04:25,250
Iron carries heat off your hand
fast; wood carries it slowly.

68
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:30,340
Steel's over 100 times faster than wood.

69
00:04:30,700 --> 00:04:35,130
Rugs and tiles are the same: tiles
carry heat from your feet faster.

70
00:04:37,433 --> 00:04:40,143
The zipper's colder than cloth, too!

71
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:45,660
But hold it a moment and it isn't
cold: your heat has gone in.

72
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:49,710
Next time something feels cold, ask:

73
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:55,050
is it really colder, or just
great at carrying heat away?

74
00:04:55,267 --> 00:04:58,100
So what blocks heat best?

75
00:04:58,100 --> 00:05:03,100
This may surprise you: the best
heat blocker around you is air.

76
00:05:04,333 --> 00:05:08,123
Air? We're soaking in air every day!

77
00:05:08,533 --> 00:05:11,513
Still air barely carries heat.

78
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:14,710
The trouble is, air hates staying still.

79
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:17,790
Why does it move?

80
00:05:17,967 --> 00:05:20,877
Air next to you gets warm and floats up,

81
00:05:21,867 --> 00:05:24,987
cold air moves in, and
heat gets carried off.

82
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:29,160
Why does warm air float up?

83
00:05:30,067 --> 00:05:32,667
It's lighter than cold air, so it rises.

84
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:37,530
Even standing still, you keep losing heat.

85
00:05:38,100 --> 00:05:41,590
So the jacket's warm
because feathers are warm?

86
00:05:42,333 --> 00:05:45,843
Down actually carries
heat about as well as air.

87
00:05:46,833 --> 00:05:49,153
Magnify a puff of down:

88
00:05:49,367 --> 00:05:53,817
it's a tangle of super-fine
threads, spreading out every way.

89
00:05:55,367 --> 00:05:59,397
Packed together, they prop
open tons of tiny spaces.

90
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:03,037
Air gets trapped in them
and can't float away.

91
00:06:04,633 --> 00:06:07,223
So why not just wear air?

92
00:06:08,133 --> 00:06:11,273
Open air swirls around and takes heat off.

93
00:06:12,200 --> 00:06:15,470
Cut it into tiny cells,
and it can't swirl.

94
00:06:16,667 --> 00:06:19,433
Lots of tiny rooms!

95
00:06:19,433 --> 00:06:22,653
A down jacket is a coat full of air!

96
00:06:23,267 --> 00:06:25,297
Here's the clever part:

97
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:29,100
the key material is air, and air is free.

98
00:06:30,167 --> 00:06:34,237
Your sweater and your quilt
use the same trick: trap air.

99
00:06:35,933 --> 00:06:37,973
And a cat's fur too?

100
00:06:38,667 --> 00:06:42,427
Yes. Animal fur traps air in it too.

101
00:06:43,333 --> 00:06:46,413
What if I squash my jacket?

102
00:06:46,767 --> 00:06:50,337
Hug it hard, and the
spot you hug goes flat.

103
00:06:50,967 --> 00:06:53,107
Flat spots trap less air,

104
00:06:54,167 --> 00:06:56,100
so less warmth.

105
00:06:56,100 --> 00:06:57,980
What if I sit on it?

106
00:06:58,867 --> 00:07:02,337
Then the bit under your
bottom is flattest.

107
00:07:02,967 --> 00:07:06,837
So heavier and thicker
always means warmer?

108
00:07:07,333 --> 00:07:09,113
Heavy isn't key.

109
00:07:09,233 --> 00:07:11,923
It's how much still air is trapped inside.

110
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:16,020
And if it gets rained on?

111
00:07:16,933 --> 00:07:19,633
Water fills those tiny spaces

112
00:07:20,033 --> 00:07:21,983
and pushes the air out.

113
00:07:22,700 --> 00:07:26,490
Water carries heat 20-some
times better than air.

114
00:07:27,267 --> 00:07:31,927
Wet clothes let heat out much
faster, so they're less warm.

115
00:07:33,300 --> 00:07:36,910
Wet down collapses; the spaces close.

116
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:39,950
Winter rain: you know that cold.

117
00:07:40,733 --> 00:07:43,313
Wet hair's freezing too!

118
00:07:44,067 --> 00:07:48,617
Yes. And as water dries, it
takes even more heat from you.

119
00:07:49,567 --> 00:07:52,787
Why is it so cold when it's windy?

120
00:07:53,267 --> 00:07:55,367
Remember that rising warm air?

121
00:07:56,567 --> 00:07:58,837
Wind blows it away first,

122
00:07:59,700 --> 00:08:03,190
cold air swaps in, heat leaves faster.

123
00:08:03,567 --> 00:08:07,407
Wind often takes heat far
faster than slow contact.

124
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:12,990
That's wind chill: same
temperature, much colder with wind.

125
00:08:14,567 --> 00:08:19,067
Wind chill is about bare skin:
your face, ears, and hands.

126
00:08:20,467 --> 00:08:23,547
No wonder my ears hurt first!

127
00:08:23,567 --> 00:08:27,717
So the jacket's outer
layer should block wind,

128
00:08:27,733 --> 00:08:29,923
and keep rain out, too.

129
00:08:30,267 --> 00:08:32,300
Like a wind wall!

130
00:08:32,300 --> 00:08:34,290
What if it's unzipped?

131
00:08:34,833 --> 00:08:38,793
Wind pours in and blows
your warm air away.

132
00:08:39,167 --> 00:08:40,897
You've felt that before.

133
00:08:41,700 --> 00:08:44,633
Oh! Flasks have no plug,

134
00:08:44,633 --> 00:08:47,700
so how does ice water stay cold?

135
00:08:47,700 --> 00:08:50,840
A flask and a down jacket do the same job.

136
00:08:51,767 --> 00:08:56,237
It doesn't heat or cool; heat
just can't easily get through.

137
00:08:57,367 --> 00:09:02,077
A flask is two bottles, with
the air between nearly all gone.

138
00:09:02,800 --> 00:09:05,440
The jacket locks air in,

139
00:09:05,500 --> 00:09:07,570
and it doesn't want any?

140
00:09:08,200 --> 00:09:12,620
Right. Jacket air can't move,
but heat still creeps by;

141
00:09:13,333 --> 00:09:17,393
the flask is nearly empty
there; heat barely crosses.

142
00:09:18,133 --> 00:09:22,100
So a flask is a super down jacket?

143
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:24,560
Sort of, except you can't wear it.

144
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:28,170
Is the neck like my open zipper?

145
00:09:28,867 --> 00:09:33,527
Pretty much. No vacuum at the
neck, so much heat slips out there.

146
00:09:34,467 --> 00:09:38,987
So it keeps hot soup
hot and ice water cold!

147
00:09:39,200 --> 00:09:43,940
Yes. Remember the rule of heat?
The flask blocks its path.

148
00:09:45,267 --> 00:09:48,367
And a down jacket in summer?

149
00:09:48,367 --> 00:09:49,787
Keeps heat in.

150
00:09:50,300 --> 00:09:53,440
In summer you don't want
that, so you'd roast.

151
00:09:54,533 --> 00:09:56,863
And our wrapped ice cube?

152
00:09:57,600 --> 00:10:01,250
It's so warm, it'll melt faster, right?

153
00:10:01,667 --> 00:10:05,057
Many guess that, but
there's no heater inside.

154
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:09,350
Remember the thermometer night?
It didn't warm up at all.

155
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:14,720
Lots of classrooms try this:
one cube wrapped, one not.

156
00:10:16,200 --> 00:10:18,200
The wrapped one melts slower.

157
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:21,790
Wrapping makes it slower?!

158
00:10:22,533 --> 00:10:23,683
Got it!

159
00:10:23,867 --> 00:10:27,127
It's warm because it keeps MY heat in!

160
00:10:28,167 --> 00:10:30,533
Wear it, wait a bit:

161
00:10:30,533 --> 00:10:33,913
that brief moment is
your heat filling it up.

162
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:36,890
So that's why!

163
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:42,160
Ice cream in a down jacket:
melts faster, or slower?

164
00:10:42,300 --> 00:10:43,770
Tell us in the comments.

165
00:10:44,833 --> 00:10:50,243
Want to find life's everyday whys
with us? Subscribe to So That's Why.
